  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Thursday)\nIssued at 141 PM CDT FRI JUL 31 2026\n\nScattered cloud cover has developed this afternoon, with a\ngrowing cumulus field along and south of a stalled boundary, from\nTroy to Tuscaloosa to Hamilton. Guidance continues to indicate\nthe development of some showers and storms later this afternoon\nand into the evening generally along this boundary. However,\noverall coverage is expected to be low. By tomorrow, very high\nprecipitable water (PWAT) values of >2.2-2.3\" will advance\nnorthward into the warm sector of an approaching trough across\nthe Central U.S.. A surface low currently over Iowa will move\nover Illinois by tomorrow, driving a cold front into the region\ntomorrow evening. Models are indicating a morning round of\ngenerally sub-severe showers and storms to sweep through the area\nbetween 7 am and noon, followed by the potential for a line of\nstrong to severe thunderstorms to move across the Ohio and\nTennessee valleys and southward, ahead of the front. This will\nbring mainly a damaging wind threat to Central Alabama starting\nduring the mid-afternoon hours and lasting toward midnight. A\nlevel 1 Marginal risk has been introduced for many of the\ncounties in the northern and western part of our area. With high\nPWATs, very heavy rainfall may occur with passing thunderstorms.\nHowever, overall rain amounts currently do not look to be enough\nto cause widescale flooding concerns.\n\nAdditional afternoon thunderstorm development is currently\nforecast for Sunday afternoon across eastern Central Alabama as\nthe entire system advances to the east. No severe threat has been\nintroduced as of this forecast cycle. The aforementioned trough\nwill get left behind in the upper Ohio Valley by the start of the\nweek, becoming a cutoff low that will have some effects on\nCentral Alabama. Namely, low to medium rain chances will be\nmaintained each afternoon through next week, predominantly across\neastern Central Alabama, in association with the upper low to the\nnorth. Additionally, we will see a bit of a break in high\ntemperatures early to mid week, with highs in the upper 80s to\nlow 90s. A ridge will then begin to build in from the west by\nlate next week, with afternoon highs climbing back into the low\nto mid 90s.\n\n12",
